package com.hpcai270

// Expanded field notes — Days 2–5
val expandedDays2to5 = listOf(
Day(2,"Inside an HPC cluster","Nodes, roles and control plane","A cluster is a collection of connected computers. A typical environment separates user access, management and compute roles.\n\nLOGIN NODE: where users prepare and submit work.\nCONTROL/MANAGEMENT: keeps cluster services coordinated.\nCOMPUTE NODE: runs workloads.\nSTORAGE: provides persistent data.","A useful mental model:\n\nUSER → LOGIN → SCHEDULER → COMPUTE NODES → STORAGE\n                         ↕\n                       NETWORK\n\nThe exact architecture varies by site. The important point is that compute, storage and management communicate through the cluster network.","NODE ANATOMY\n\nA compute node may contain CPUs, RAM, local NVMe, one or more GPUs, high-speed NICs/HCAs and a Linux operating system.\n\nGPU nodes add accelerators and often need very fast networking for multi-node jobs.","WHY THE NETWORK MATTERS\n\nImagine 8 GPU servers training one model. They repeatedly exchange data and synchronization messages. The network is therefore not merely “connectivity”; latency, bandwidth and congestion can affect application performance.","STORAGE VIEW\n\nShared storage lets many compute nodes access common datasets and results. Local NVMe can provide very fast scratch space. HPC designs often combine shared parallel storage with local temporary storage.","TOOLS TO RECOGNIZE\n\nLinux: ssh, top, free, lsblk, ip\nScheduler: Slurm\nStorage/performance: df, mount, iostat, fio\nNetworking: ip, ethtool, ss\n\nLater we will use these for real troubleshooting.","TASK\n\nSketch LOGIN, SCHEDULER, 3 COMPUTE NODES and STORAGE. Add the network around them.\n\nINTERVIEW: What is a compute node? Why separate login and compute roles? Why can local NVMe and shared storage coexist?",pages=listOf("A cluster is a collection of connected computers. Typical roles include LOGIN, MANAGEMENT, COMPUTE and STORAGE.\n\nLOGIN: prepare/submit work.\nMANAGEMENT: coordinate services.\nCOMPUTE: run workloads.\nSTORAGE: provide persistent data.","ARCHITECTURE\n\nUSER → LOGIN → SCHEDULER → COMPUTE NODES\n                         ↓\n                      STORAGE\n\nNETWORK connects the environment. Exact designs vary by site.","COMPUTE NODE ANATOMY\n\nCPU + RAM + local NVMe + GPU(s) + high-speed NIC/HCA + Linux.\n\nGPU nodes add accelerators and often require high-speed node-to-node communication.","WHY NETWORKING MATTERS\n\nMultiple GPU servers may exchange data repeatedly during distributed training. Bandwidth, latency and congestion can therefore affect application performance.","STORAGE VIEW\n\nShared storage gives many nodes common access to datasets and results. Local NVMe can provide very fast scratch space. HPC systems can use both.","TOOLS\n\nLinux: ssh, top, free, lsblk, ip\nSlurm: srun, sbatch, squeue\nStorage: df, mount, iostat, fio\nNetworking: ip, ss, ethtool","TASK + INTERVIEW\n\nDraw LOGIN → SCHEDULER → 3 COMPUTE NODES → STORAGE with a network around it.\n\nExplain compute node, login node, shared storage and local scratch.")),
Day(3,"CPU vs GPU","Why AI loves parallel compute","A CPU is a general-purpose processor designed for varied control-heavy work. A GPU contains many execution units designed to process large numbers of similar operations efficiently.\n\nNeither is simply “better”: workload characteristics decide which is appropriate.","AI TRAINING\n\nNeural-network workloads perform enormous numbers of matrix and tensor operations. GPUs are optimized for this style of parallel computation and have specialized memory and acceleration features.","GPU MEMORY\n\nThe GPU needs data close to the compute engines. A simplified path is:\n\nStorage → network → host memory → GPU memory → GPU compute\n\nEvery transfer has latency and bandwidth limits.","GPU STARVATION\n\nIf computation finishes faster than the next data arrives, the accelerator waits. Increasing GPU count will not automatically fix an I/O bottleneck.\n\nPerformance engineering looks at the entire pipeline.","MULTI-GPU / MULTI-NODE\n\nOne model can use multiple GPUs in one server or across many servers. Communication libraries such as NCCL help coordinate GPU collectives. High-speed fabrics become increasingly important as scale grows.","REAL PRODUCTS\n\nNVIDIA H200 and Blackwell platforms are examples of modern AI accelerators. AMD Instinct is another accelerator family. Exact system designs differ by vendor and workload.","TASK\n\nExplain the difference between CPU and GPU without saying “GPU is faster.” Then draw Storage → Network → GPU memory → GPU compute.\n\nINTERVIEW: What is GPU starvation? Why does storage affect AI training?",pages=listOf("CPU = flexible general-purpose compute. GPU = highly parallel accelerator. Neither is universally better; workload characteristics matter.","AI WORKLOAD\n\nNeural networks perform huge numbers of matrix/tensor operations. GPUs are designed to execute this type of parallel work efficiently.","DATA PATH\n\nStorage → network → host memory → GPU memory → GPU compute\n\nTransfers have bandwidth and latency limits.","GPU STARVATION\n\nIf data cannot arrive fast enough, the GPU waits. Adding more GPUs does not automatically solve an I/O bottleneck.","SCALE\n\nMulti-GPU and multi-node training requires communication among accelerators. NCCL is a key NVIDIA collective-communication library; fast fabrics become important at scale.","PRODUCTS\n\nRecognize NVIDIA H200 and Blackwell platforms and AMD Instinct as modern accelerator families. System details vary.","TASK + INTERVIEW\n\nExplain CPU vs GPU without saying “GPU is faster.” Draw the data path. Define GPU starvation and explain why storage can affect training.")),
Day(4,"HPC software stack","From hardware to application","HPC is a layered system. Hardware alone does not create an HPC environment. Linux, drivers, libraries, schedulers, filesystems and applications must work together.","STACK\n\nAPPLICATION\n↓\nMPI / OpenMP / AI libraries\n↓\nSCHEDULER + SYSTEM SERVICES\n↓\nFILESYSTEM + NETWORK\n↓\nDRIVERS / FIRMWARE\n↓\nCPU / GPU / NIC / STORAGE\n\nA failure or bottleneck in one layer can surface as a problem somewhere else.","MPI vs OPENMP\n\nOpenMP commonly parallelizes work within a shared-memory node. MPI coordinates processes that can run across nodes. Real applications may use both.","MODULES\n\nHPC environments often provide multiple compiler, MPI and library versions. Environment modules help users select compatible software stacks without permanently changing the whole system.","SCHEDULER\n\nSlurm can allocate CPUs, GPUs, memory and time. Users request resources; the scheduler places jobs according to policy and availability.","STORAGE LAYER\n\nThe application may see a filesystem such as NFS, Lustre, IBM Storage Scale or CephFS. Underneath are storage servers, network paths and media. Later lessons will dissect these layers.","TASK\n\nDraw the seven-layer stack from application to hardware. Mark which layers you already know from enterprise storage and which are new.\n\nINTERVIEW: Why can a storage problem look like an application problem?",pages=listOf("HPC is a layered system. Hardware alone is not enough: Linux, drivers, libraries, schedulers, filesystems and applications must cooperate.","STACK\n\nAPPLICATION\n↓\nMPI / OpenMP / AI libraries\n↓\nSCHEDULER + SERVICES\n↓\nFILESYSTEM + NETWORK\n↓\nDRIVERS / FIRMWARE\n↓\nCPU / GPU / NIC / STORAGE","MPI + OPENMP\n\nOpenMP commonly parallelizes work within one shared-memory node. MPI coordinates processes across nodes. Applications can combine both.","SOFTWARE MODULES\n\nHPC sites often have multiple compiler, MPI and library versions. Environment modules let users select a compatible stack.","SCHEDULER\n\nSlurm allocates resources such as CPU, GPU, memory and time. Users request resources rather than manually picking arbitrary servers.","STORAGE LAYER\n\nApplications may use NFS, Lustre, IBM Storage Scale or CephFS. Underneath are network paths, servers and media.","TASK + INTERVIEW\n\nDraw the stack. Identify layers you know and layers you need to learn. Explain why a storage fault can appear as an application slowdown.")),
Day(5,"What is a job?","The scheduler's world","An HPC job is a request to perform work using cluster resources. Instead of logging into a random compute server and consuming resources, a user describes what the workload needs.","RESOURCE REQUEST\n\nA job can request:\n• CPU cores\n• GPUs\n• RAM\n• wall-clock time\n• number of nodes\n• constraints/partitions\n\nThe scheduler considers availability and policy before starting it.","SLURM BASICS\n\nsbatch = submit a batch job\nsqueue = view jobs\nsinfo = inspect cluster/partitions\nscancel = cancel a job\nsrun = launch work\n\nThese commands will become practical skills later.","JOB LIFECYCLE\n\nSUBMIT → PENDING → RUNNING → COMPLETING → COMPLETED\n\nA pending job is not necessarily broken. It may simply be waiting for the requested resources or policy conditions.","WHY STORAGE ADMINS CARE\n\nA job can be correctly scheduled and still perform badly because data access is slow. Storage, filesystem, network and compute performance must be considered together.","AI EXAMPLE\n\nA distributed training job might request multiple GPU nodes, large memory and many hours. During the run it repeatedly reads training data and writes checkpoints.","TASK\n\nWrite a pretend AI job request: nodes, GPUs, CPUs, RAM and time. Then explain each field.\n\nINTERVIEW: What is the difference between a job and a node? Why might a job remain pending?",pages=listOf("An HPC job is a request to perform work using cluster resources. Users describe workload requirements instead of choosing arbitrary compute servers.","RESOURCE REQUEST\n\nCPU cores • GPUs • RAM • wall-clock time • node count • partition/constraints\n\nThe scheduler evaluates availability and policy.","SLURM\n\nsbatch submit\nsqueue view jobs\nsinfo inspect partitions\nscancel cancel\nsrun launch work","JOB LIFECYCLE\n\nSUBMIT → PENDING → RUNNING → COMPLETING → COMPLETED\n\nPENDING can simply mean the requested resources are not currently available.","STORAGE CONNECTION\n\nA job can be scheduled correctly but still run slowly because filesystem, storage or network I/O is a bottleneck.","AI JOB\n\nA training job may request multiple GPU nodes, large memory and many hours. It repeatedly reads datasets and writes checkpoints.","TASK + INTERVIEW\n\nWrite a pretend AI resource request. Explain job vs node and give two reasons a job might remain pending."))
)
